 "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_30", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_31", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "All 12 reviewed nodes share one contact pool up to t~60074 and a nearly disjoint pool afterwards, with a short quiet transition band (~59075-61074) in which both pools and the group are almost silent. Old partners are inactive graph-wide after the change point and new partners inactive before it, ruling out popularity/volume explanations; aggregated (time-ignoring) connections such as 356-1526 exist but are confined entirely to the pre-switch regime."}, "raw_final_response": "**Conclusion**\n\nThe group's partner set flips almost completely at t \u2248 60,000:\n\n- Before (55075\u201360074), all 12 members contact a shared pool {61, 63, 116, 239, 349, 749, 912, 918, 926, 1110, 1526, 1593, 1760, 1792, 1922} (steps 06, 10, 12, 14, 18, 20, 22, 24).\n- After (60075\u201365074), the same members contact a disjoint pool {40, 80, 184, 250, 327, 338, 493, 515, 785, 897, 924, 1348, 1355, 1505, 1750} (steps 07, 11, 13, 15, 19, 21, 23, 25). Jaccard overlap \u2248 0.\n- The switch is abrupt, not gradual: the old pool logs 21 events in 58075\u201359074 then ~1 in the next 3000 units (step 16), while the new pool is silent until it bursts with 31 events in 61075\u201362074 (step 17); the group itself is nearly silent in the 2000-unit transition band (step 28).\n- Confounder controls: old partners are essentially inactive graph-wide after the switch (step 26) and new partners essentially inactive before it (step 27), so this is not \"popular hubs always present\"; group volume is roughly flat across the period (step 03), so it is not a volume artefact; and none of the group are top-active nodes (step 30), so the pattern is not driven by hub dominance.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_107b45e5\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [114, 140, 182, 293, 356, 654, 731, 959, 1107, 1260, 1268, 1311],\n  \"window\": [55074, 65074],\n  \"confidence\": 0.91,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_18\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_19\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_20\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_21\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_22\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_23\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_24\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_25\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_17\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_28\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_26\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_27\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_30\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_31\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"All 12 reviewed nodes share one contact pool up to t~60074 and a nearly disjoint pool afterwards, with a short quiet transition band (~59075-61074) in which both pools and the group are almost silent. Old partners are inactive graph-wide after the change point and new partners inactive before it, ruling out popularity/volume explanations; aggregated (time-ignoring) connections such as 356-1526 exist but are confined entirely to the pre-switch regime.\"\n}